to court with a verdict. They found James Perry guilty on each of the three counts of first-degree murder. Judge D. Warren Donahue imposed three death sentences and an additional sentence of life in prison for conspiring to commit murder. Six months later, in April 1996, it was finally time for Lawrence Horn to face trial for masterminding the murders. As was carefully planned by investigators, testimony Lawrence gave in the civil suit was presented in court, proving he was a liar. Despite what Lawrence claimed, hundreds of pieces of evidence linked him directly to James Perry. His cousin, Thomas Turner, was called to give evidence in the trial. Turner told of introducing Lawrence and Perry and later acting as their go-between when they

